  "interpretation": "The patch refactors `app/db.py` to return plain Python dictionaries instead of Pydantic `Note` models, aiming to reduce serialization overhead. Validation passed, confirming API contract compliance. However, the runner rejected the change due to insufficient improvement. The candidate mean latency (0.4988 ms) is marginally lower than the parent (0.5023 ms), yielding a relative improvement of only ~0.7%. This delta falls within the observed noise coefficient of variation (~0.49%), suggesting the change is statistically indistinguishable from baseline variance. While `list_search` mean latency decreased slightly (0.5947 vs 0.5990 ms), the `get` operation mean latency increased (0.3796 vs 0.3841 ms is actually a decrease, wait: parent get mean 0.3841, candidate get mean 0.3796, so it decreased). Actually, parent get mean is 0.3841, candidate is 0.3796. Parent list_search mean is 0.5990, candidate is 0.5947. All operations show slight decreases. The rejection is based on the aggregate relative improvement being too small to justify the risk or effort, as the noise floor obscures any definitive gain. The hypothesis that Pydantic overhead was the bottleneck appears incorrect or negligible in this specific workload context.",
  "limitations": [
    "Relative improvement (0.7%) is within the noise coefficient of variation (~0.5%), indicating lack of statistical significance.",
    "Interpretation relies on aggregate mean/median metrics; individual request distribution tails are not analyzed.",
    "No confidence intervals or p-values are provided to quantify the certainty of the latency difference.",
    "The analysis does not account for potential long-term memory usage or scalability impacts of the refactoring."
